course: geography
lesson: 5
title: Continents and oceans
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Look at a globe from below and you see mostly blue: oceans cover about 71 percent of the Earth, and all the land together is less than a third.
explain: The land is split into continents, but how many depends on who is counting: seven in Britain and America, six where one America is taught, five rings on the Olympic flag.
explain: That is a human agreement, like the EU5 designers deciding where one region ends and the next begins; Europe and Asia are really one landmass, Eurasia.
explain: The oceans are really one world ocean too, given five names, just as the game cuts the sea into many sea zones for moving fleets.
explain: The Pacific alone is bigger than all the land on Earth, and its deepest point, the Challenger Deep, could swallow Mount Everest with over 2 km of water to spare.
fact: Children in Britain, the United States and Australia usually learn seven continents: Africa, Antarctica, Asia, Australia or Oceania, Europe, North America and South America.
fact: In many Spanish-speaking and Portuguese-speaking countries, children learn one America, giving six continents, and the Olympic rings stand for five inhabited continents.
fact: Asia is the largest continent, with about 44 million square kilometres, almost a third of all land, and nearly six in ten of the world's people; India and China alone hold more than a third of all people.
fact: Europe and Asia are one landmass, often called Eurasia, and the usual boundary follows the Ural Mountains, the Ural River, the Caspian Sea, the Caucasus, the Black Sea and the Bosporus, a narrow strait; the city of Istanbul lies on both sides of it, partly in Europe and partly in Asia.
fact: Africa is the second largest continent, with about a fifth of the world's land, and it lies on both sides of the equator.
fact: Australia is the smallest continent, with about 7.7 million square kilometres, and it is the only continent that is a single country; it is such a big landmass on its own that it is counted as a continent, not just an island.
fact: The five oceans are the Pacific, the Atlantic, the Indian, the Southern and the Arctic, and National Geographic began marking the Southern Ocean, which surrounds Antarctica, on its maps in 2021.
fact: The Pacific Ocean covers about 165 million square kilometres, which is more than all the land on Earth put together; Magellan named it Pacific, "peaceful", after a calm crossing in 1520, but it has fierce storms too.
fact: Oceans cover about 71 percent of the Earth's surface, so less than a third is land.
fact: The deepest known point of the ocean is the Challenger Deep in the Mariana Trench in the Pacific, about 10,900 metres down.
fact: About two-thirds of the Earth's land lies in the northern hemisphere.
fact: Antarctica has no permanent residents, only scientists and staff who stay for a season or a year.
fact: Portuguese ships spent decades creeping down Africa's coast before Bartolomeu Dias rounded the Cape of Good Hope in 1488.
exercise: How many continents do children in Britain usually learn? | Seven | Five | Six | Eight
exercise: Why might a child in Mexico say there are six continents? | She learns North and South America as one America | Mexico does not count Antarctica | Mexico counts Europe and Asia as one | She has never seen a globe
exercise: What do the five Olympic rings stand for? | Five inhabited continents | Five oceans | The five first Olympic cities | Five colours of flags
exercise: Which is the largest continent? | Asia | Africa | North America | Antarctica
exercise: About what share of the world's people live in Asia? | Nearly six in ten | About one in ten | Exactly half | About one in four
exercise: What is the landmass of Europe and Asia together often called? | Eurasia | Euroasia Minor | Pangaea | Asiana
exercise: Which mountains usually mark the boundary between Europe and Asia? | The Ural Mountains | The Alps | The Carpathian Mountains | The Himalayas
exercise: Which is the second largest continent? | Africa | Europe | South America | Australia
exercise: Which continent is a single country? | Australia | Antarctica | Africa | Europe
exercise: Name the five oceans. | Pacific, Atlantic, Indian, Southern and Arctic | Pacific, Atlantic, Indian, Mediterranean and Arctic | Pacific, Atlantic, Caribbean, Southern and Arctic | Pacific, Atlantic, Indian, Antarctic and Baltic
exercise: When did National Geographic begin marking the Southern Ocean? | In 2021 | In 1884 | In 1569 | In 1998
exercise: Is the Pacific larger or smaller than all the land on Earth together? | Larger | Smaller | About half the size | Exactly the same
exercise: Roughly what fraction of the Earth's surface is land? | Less than a third | About half | About two-thirds | About nine-tenths
exercise: Where is the deepest known point of the ocean? | The Challenger Deep in the Pacific | The middle of the Atlantic Ocean, near the ridge | Under the North Pole | The Dead Sea
exercise: Which is greater, the height of Everest or the depth of the Challenger Deep? | The depth of the Challenger Deep | The height of Everest | They are exactly equal | Nobody has measured them
exercise: In which hemisphere lies most of the Earth's land? | The northern hemisphere | The southern hemisphere | Exactly half in each | The western hemisphere only
exercise: Which continent has no permanent residents? | Antarctica | Australia | South America | Europe
test: Mate reckons there's exactly seven continents and that's a scientific fact. Right? | No, how many is an agreement and differs by country | Yes, scientists fixed it in 1884 | Yes, every country teaches seven | No, there are really twelve
test: Which city lies partly in Europe and partly in Asia? | Istanbul | Rome | Cairo | Saint Petersburg
test: In EU5 the Ottomans hold lands on both sides of the Bosporus. What does that real strait separate? | Europe from Asia | Africa from western Asia | Europe from Africa | North from South America
test: Which two Asian countries together hold more than a third of all humans? | India and China | Japan and Korea | Indonesia and Iran | Russia and Mongolia
test: Why is Australia called a continent and not just the biggest island? | It is a very big landmass on its own, so it is counted as a continent | It is bigger than Asia | Islands must be in the Pacific | Its name was chosen by Captain Cook, who wanted it called a continent and not an island
test: Which ocean surrounds Antarctica? | The Southern Ocean | The Arctic Ocean | The Indian Ocean only | The Atlantic Ocean only
test: Which hemisphere has more ocean? | The southern hemisphere | The northern hemisphere | They have exactly the same | Neither, the oceans are on the equator
test: If Everest were dropped into the Challenger Deep, what would happen? | Its peak would still be more than 2 km under water | Its peak would stick out by 2 km | It would fill the trench exactly | It would float
test: In EU5 you send Portugal down the African coast. How long did the real Portuguese take to reach the Cape of Good Hope? | Decades of voyages, until Dias rounded it in 1488 | A single summer in 1337 | It was never reached | Just one voyage in 1600
test: the pacific is called the peaceful ocean so it must be calm innit? | No, Magellan named it on a calm crossing, but it has fierce storms | Yes, it never has storms | Yes, it is too big for wind | No, it is called that because it is warm
test: Why do most of the world's people and the EU5 map's great powers lie in the north? | About two-thirds of the land is in the northern hemisphere | The south is all ice and cold deserts | The south has no rivers | The game hides the southern lands from the players on purpose
exam: Identify the continent of the greatest extent and the most numerous population. | Asia | Africa | Europe | North America
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the proportion of the terrestrial surface covered by the oceans? | Approximately seventy-one per cent | Approximately fifty per cent | Approximately twenty-nine per cent | Approximately ninety per cent
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the number of continents. | It varies according to the convention adopted | It is invariably seven | It is invariably five | It is invariably six
exam: Ascertain the location of the greatest known oceanic depth. | The Challenger Deep in the Mariana Trench | The Puerto Rico Trench in the Atlantic | The Mediterranean basin | The Arctic Ocean beneath the Pole
